        "format": "tikz",
        "code": "% mathsmap-diagram-colours {\"version\":1,\"kind\":\"geometry\",\"base\":[],\"semantic\":[],\"reference\":\"source page 49 p49-q16-diagram\"}\n\\usetikzlibrary{calc,angles,quotes}\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,draw=black,text=black,line width=0.8pt,font=\\fontsize{10}{12}\\selectfont]\n\\special{dvisvgm:raw <metadata data-graph-strokes=\"1\"/>}\n\\coordinate (B) at (0,0);\n\\coordinate (C) at (2.6,0);\n\\coordinate (D) at (3.9,0);\n\\coordinate (A) at (1.3,3.4);\n\\draw (B)--(A)--(C)--(B);\n\\draw (C)--(D);\n\\draw[line width=0.4pt] ($(B)!0.5!(A)!3pt!90:(A)$)--($(B)!0.5!(A)!3pt!-90:(A)$);\n\\draw[line width=0.4pt] ($(A)!0.5!(C)!3pt!90:(C)$)--($(A)!0.5!(C)!3pt!-90:(C)$);\n\\pic[draw,line width=0.4pt,angle radius=5mm,angle eccentricity=1.4,\"$x$\"] {angle=C--B--A};\n\\pic[draw,line width=0.4pt,angle radius=4mm,angle eccentricity=1.6,\"$y$\"] {angle=D--C--A};\n\\node[above=3pt,inner sep=1pt] at (A) {$A$};\n\\node[below left=2pt,inner sep=1pt] at (B) {$B$};\n\\node[below=3pt,inner sep=1pt] at (C) {$C$};\n\\node[right=3pt,inner sep=1pt] at (D) {$D$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}",
        "src": null,
        "widthMm": 50,
        "alt": "Mathematical diagram",
        "overlayOf": null,
        "transparent": false,
        "axes": null,
        "spec": {
          "sourcePage": 49,
          "sourceRegion": "Question 16, shared diagram to the left of parts a and b",
          "description": "Isosceles triangle ABC with A above BC, AB and AC marked equal, and BC extended through C to D. Interior angle ABC is x and exterior angle ACD is y; both have arcs.",
          "mathematics": "AB = AC; B, C and D are collinear in that order. Therefore angle ABC equals angle ACB, and angle ACB plus angle ACD is 180 degrees.",
          "answerVisibility": "Only the source givens x and y are visible; no derived angles or proof steps appear in the question diagram."
        },
        "derived": false,
        "reviewStatus": "needs-review"
      }
    ],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p49-q16-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Prove that $y=180^{\\circ}-x$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\angle ACB=x$ because the base angles of an isosceles triangle are equal. Since $x+y=180^{\\circ}$ on the straight line $BCD$, $y=180^{\\circ}-x$.",
          "worked": "$AB=AC$, so the base angles of the isosceles triangle are equal:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\angle ACB&=x.\\end{align*}$$\nAdjacent angles on the straight line $BCD$ add to $180^{\\circ}$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\angle ACB+\\angle ACD&=180^{\\circ}\\\\x+y&=180^{\\circ}\\\\y&=180^{\\circ}-x.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      },
      {
        "id": "p49-q16-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Prove that $\\angle BAC=180^{\\circ}-2x$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The equal base angles are both $x$. By the triangle angle sum, $\\angle BAC+x+x=180^{\\circ}$, so $\\angle BAC=180^{\\circ}-2x$.",
          "worked": "From part (a), $\\angle ABC=\\angle ACB=x$. The angles in triangle $ABC$ add to $180^{\\circ}$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\angle BAC+\\angle ABC+\\angle ACB&=180^{\\circ}\\\\\\angle BAC+x+x&=180^{\\circ}\\\\\\angle BAC+2x&=180^{\\circ}\\\\\\angle BAC&=180^{\\circ}-2x.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 34
      }
    ]
  },
